21:36:46 [a082] --- LANGUAGE GUIDANCE (knowledge base) ---
21:36:46 [a082] Target level: age 8, approx MLU 11+ words. Match question wording to this length.
21:36:46 [a082] 
21:36:46 [a082] Use this conceptual-difficulty guidance to choose WHAT the questions test (content and reasoning level):
21:36:46 [a082] --- CONCEPT GUIDANCE (knowledge base) ---
21:36:46 [a082] Concept level (ages 8): School-age conceptual reasoning involving explicit category relations, cross-classification, hierarchical reasoning, causal explanation, food-origin/processing reasoning, problem-solution reasoning, and structured narrative understanding.
21:36:46 [a082] Wording: Questions can be longer and can ask for explanation, comparison, or reasoning, but should remain concrete and contextually grounded. Phrase every question as ONE simple spoken clause a child would hear in conversation; split complex ideas into the question frames below instead of packing them into one sentence.
21:36:46 [a082] Question length: 8-14 words when expressive language level supports it.
21:36:46 [a082] Avoid: fully abstract reasoning without concrete context; adult-level nutrition or biology mechanisms; technical food production; highly ambiguous category exceptions; complex social inference without story support; academic connector phrases such as 'in terms of', 'with respect to', 'in what way'; two asks packed into one question (e.g. 'How are X and Y alike in terms of what they are made from?' — use 'What are X and Y both made from?' instead)
21:36:46 [a082] Use these simple question frames (one clause each): How are X and Y alike? | Why does X belong to this category? | Can X belong to two categories? | What is X made from? | Why did the character do that? | What happened after X? | What should the character do next?
21:36:46 [a082] Target these concepts (vary across the question set):
21:36:46 [a082] - within food category: Ask whether a food belongs to a specific food category. e.g. Is an apple a fruit?
21:36:46 [a082] - typicality reasoning: Use typical examples for simple quizzes; use less typical examples only for advanced reasoning or discussion. e.g. Is an apple a fruit?
21:36:46 [a082] - cross classification: Ask whether one item can be classified in multiple valid ways. e.g. Is an apple both fruit and food?
21:36:46 [a082] - superordinate subordinate reasoning: Ask whether an item is a kind of another category, or whether one category is broader than another. e.g. Is a poodle a kind of dog?
21:36:46 [a082] - origin and processing reasoning: Ask about natural source, animal source, ingredient source, or simple processing pathway. e.g. Do apples grow on trees?
21:36:46 [a082] - causal explanation: Ask why something happens or why an object/action is useful. e.g. Why do we use umbrellas?
21:36:46 [a082] - familiar event script: Ask about common actions, objects, people, or places in familiar events. e.g. Do books go in backpacks?
21:36:46 [a082] - event sequence and planning: Ask whether an action happens before, after, or to prepare for a familiar event. e.g. Do we pack before school?
21:36:46 [a082] Familiar items per topic: Fruit: apple, banana, orange, grape, strawberry | School: backpack, book, pencil, teacher, classroom, paper | Home: bed, cup, table, chair, toothbrush, clothes | Food: bread, milk, cheese, eggs, flour, pasta | Nature: tree, flower, bird, fish, rain, sun
21:36:46 [a082] Example question at this level: How are apples and bananas alike?
21:36:46 [a082] Example question at this level: Is an apple both fruit and food?
21:36:46 [a082] Example question at this level: Is a poodle a kind of dog?
